{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Vision.</b> Prophecy." "<b>Hard.</b> Against Babylon." "<b>It was told to me.</b> The words of the prophet." "<b>[The betrayer, etc.]</b> Whoever wishes to rebel and betray is able, and whoever wishes to be a plunderer is able to plunder." "<b>Upon Elam.</b> It is the province of Persia." "<b>Siege (צורי).</b> Like וצרת עליה (Deuteronomy 20:12), and the word comes with millera stress like שובי נפשי (Psalms 116:7)." "<b>All her sighing I have ended.</b> Some say: the sighing of Israel. And some say: there is none who will sigh over Babylon. And some say: all the sighing she made has ceased. In my opinion, from the beginning of כל אנחתה to the end of the section are the words of the prophet in the mouth of King Belshazzar, for everyone knows that the prophet rejoices in Babylon's downfall, so how could he say על כן מלאו [verse 3]? And so the sense fits together: the king said, when I heard a sound upon Elam, in the manner of men of war to mention the name of their land — Babylon's sighing I have ended with my kinds of song, for the vessels of the house of YHWH were brought and he drank from them, while Babylon is under siege and distress."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {6 "The subject of the feminine singular verb השבתיה ('I have ended it') is read as Belshazzar speaking; the prophet speaks 'in the mouth of the king,' meaning these are words he puts in the king's mouth."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:54:31.152447916Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}